1
00:00:12,115 --> 00:00:13,896
Grandpa, hurry up!

2
00:00:14,108 --> 00:00:15,932
It's still too early.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Bye, Mr. Station Attendant! We're off!

4
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:27,000
A mid-size private railway — the epitome of job security.

5
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:33,000
I was thrilled to get hired here.

6
00:00:33,475 --> 00:00:37,973
...or so it was supposed to be.

7
00:00:38,766 --> 00:00:40,366
I have a bad feeling about this...

8
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:44,000
A deadhead train will shortly arrive at Platform 1.

9
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:47,000
Well, I've only just been assigned here.

10
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:50,000
I'm sure it's nothing. Just my imagination.

11
00:00:50,275 --> 00:00:55,000
Like that sun up there,
a bright and secure future stretches out before me...

12
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:06,500
What a wreck!

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,313
...What on earth is this?

14
00:01:08,413 --> 00:01:11,000
They got an old train from the National Railways, apparently.

15
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:15,000
I can't believe they fixed it up and got it running again.

16
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:18,000
Huh... so this is what "repaired" looks like...

17
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:22,000
Ah, right. That's our company motto.

18
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:25,666
Mottainai — fix it and it'll run forever.

19
00:01:25,706 --> 00:01:26,999
Enough talk, get on!

20
00:01:27,086 --> 00:01:28,880
Yes, ma'am!

21
00:01:30,633 --> 00:01:31,913
Engage at Warp 2!

22
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:44,053
Ideally, my first day of work would involve
something easy, like being a couch potato.

23
00:02:44,850 --> 00:02:46,000
But instead...

24
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,000
It's been a year since XP's support expired!

25
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:52,000
We need to replace it immediately!

26
00:02:52,850 --> 00:02:55,000
But it still works, doesn't it?

27
00:02:55,850 --> 00:02:59,000
But that's like going into battle with no shields!

28
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:02,000
But we don't have any money.

29
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:04,500
We're at our limit here!

30
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:07,826
You read Tarumi's report, didn't you?

31
00:03:10,850 --> 00:03:15,970
...I read it. But the board says
antivirus software should be enough.

32
00:03:15,995 --> 00:03:18,446
That's what the executives think.

33
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:20,460
...I worked so hard on that report!

34
00:03:22,186 --> 00:03:24,532
He says once a decision is made, it can't be changed.

35
00:03:26,283 --> 00:03:28,936
I'm the lowest-ranking member on the board, you know.

36
00:03:29,146 --> 00:03:31,646
Well... do your best.

37
00:03:33,188 --> 00:03:34,302
*sigh*...

38
00:03:35,850 --> 00:03:39,000
Let's just hope nothing happens...

39
00:03:41,549 --> 00:03:44,830
Waaah! Akane!

40
00:03:45,001 --> 00:03:47,000
...It happened.

41
00:03:59,125 --> 00:04:01,000
Report!

42
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:06,000
We're being flooded with virus infection reports!

43
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,000
PR department, Sales, International Affairs—

44
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:16,000
Everywhere except our division.

45
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,500
What's the cause?

46
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Someone in PR.

47
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:25,500
They fell for a fake website
that told them their computer was infected,

48
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:29,000
and installed a fake antivirus program.

49
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:33,000
Which, of course, was itself the virus.

50
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:36,000
I can't believe someone actually fell for that...

51
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:39,000
But with it spreading this much—

52
00:04:40,225 --> 00:04:43,000
Why hasn't our division been infected?

53
00:04:43,475 --> 00:04:46,000
Because we're running Windows 7 and Linux.

54
00:04:46,150 --> 00:04:50,000
Indeed, only the XP machines seem to be affected.

55
00:05:01,750 --> 00:05:05,000
I see... a virus targeting XP...

56
00:05:07,001 --> 00:05:10,000
But we had every precaution in place.

57
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:13,000
It appears to be a new strain.

58
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:16,000
The antivirus software can't touch it.

59
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:22,000
This virus exploits an XP vulnerability
to seize administrator privileges.

60
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:25,000
No countermeasure can stop it.

61
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:27,500
Lt. Commander, any patches available?

62
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:29,000
None.

63
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:34,000
Of course not. Support ended ages ago.

64
00:05:34,300 --> 00:05:37,000
Our internal LAN is crawling with XP machines.

65
00:05:37,150 --> 00:05:42,000
No choice then. Let's isolate the entire
internal LAN from the backbone network.

66
00:05:42,500 --> 00:05:44,000
Lt. Commander?

67
00:05:45,850 --> 00:05:47,000
I'll have it done in five minutes.

68
00:05:47,650 --> 00:05:50,000
Make it one.

69
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:53,000
Aye aye, sir!

70
00:05:57,525 --> 00:05:59,000
System Division, hello?

71
00:06:01,050 --> 00:06:05,000
The server load is abnormally high.

72
00:06:05,050 --> 00:06:07,000
The servers are infected too?!

73
00:06:07,150 --> 00:06:09,000
Looks like it.

74
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:11,000
We hadn't applied this month's updates yet.

75
00:06:11,750 --> 00:06:13,000
That slipped through the cracks.

76
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:16,000
This is bad...

77
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,000
System Division, hello?

78
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:21,000
Now what?!

79
00:06:21,950 --> 00:06:24,000
What the—!

80
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:35,000
A hacker prank?

81
00:06:35,050 --> 00:06:37,000
Is the CTC server safe?!

82
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:39,000
If it's compromised, the signal system could—

83
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:41,000
It's fine.

84
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:44,000
It's so old that the virus doesn't affect it.

85
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:48,000
20th-century technology, by the looks of it.

86
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:50,000
Fascinating.

87
00:06:57,850 --> 00:07:03,000
But if this is a deliberate attack
using a remote-access trojan...

88
00:07:30,575 --> 00:07:34,000
...We need to disconnect from the internet.

89
00:07:34,250 --> 00:07:36,000
The public-facing web servers too?

90
00:07:36,550 --> 00:07:42,000
Yes. We need to cut off the virus
from receiving the hacker's commands.

91
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:45,000
The last resort, then.

92
00:07:49,250 --> 00:07:51,000
Tarumi, find the wiring diagrams.

93
00:07:51,250 --> 00:07:54,000
Hosono, contact PR and the CIO.

94
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:56,000
We'll handle the disconnection.

95
00:07:56,300 --> 00:08:00,000
Let's move, people!

96
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:02,500
Absolutely not!

97
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:04,500
CIO!

98
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Do you realize the losses?
If the reservation site goes down—

99
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:13,000
But this is an emergency! A data breach!
A train accident! Anything could happen!

100
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:16,000
What will the other executives say...

101
00:08:16,150 --> 00:08:19,000
No. The website stays up.

102
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:22,000
Keep it running and lock the hackers out.

103
00:08:22,100 --> 00:08:28,000
This is exactly the point!
If we had upgraded from XP,

104
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:31,000
we could have stopped this at the first stage!

105
00:08:31,750 --> 00:08:38,000
Are you really going to keep
making the same mistake?

106
00:08:38,001 --> 00:08:40,000
Now, now, let's all calm down...

107
00:08:42,001 --> 00:08:43,500
Ah—

108
00:08:43,550 --> 00:08:47,000
The database server is under attack!

109
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:49,000
We're out of time!

110
00:08:49,850 --> 00:08:54,000
I said no, and I mean no!

111
00:08:54,450 --> 00:08:58,000
Lt. Commander, can you block them with the firewall?

112
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:02,000
The firewall isn't responding...
Our communications are being jammed.

113
00:09:02,650 --> 00:09:06,000
CIO!

114
00:09:06,250 --> 00:09:08,000
This is pointless.

115
00:09:24,025 --> 00:09:27,000
Backbone router... backbone router...

116
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:30,000
VRRP configuration... there.

117
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:34,000
OpenFlow controller... got it.

118
00:09:38,175 --> 00:09:40,000
Hello, this is Hosono.

119
00:09:40,001 --> 00:09:41,300
Wh-What do you think you're doing?!

120
00:09:41,300 --> 00:09:45,000
I am about to completely sever
our connection to the internet.

121
00:09:45,750 --> 00:09:48,000
Are you insane?! Stop!

122
00:09:48,425 --> 00:09:50,000
It's only a matter of time.

123
00:09:50,150 --> 00:09:55,000
The moment even one machine is compromised, it's game over.
At this point, it's too late for half-measures.

124
00:09:55,500 --> 00:10:01,000
And if personal data gets leaked,
who takes the blame?

125
00:10:01,250 --> 00:10:05,000
Those executives you're so afraid of?

126
00:10:06,050 --> 00:10:09,000
It'll be me and the System Division, obviously!

127
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:13,000
Exactly. So the outcome is the same either way, isn't it?

128
00:10:13,001 --> 00:10:15,000
They'll pin all the blame on you alone!

129
00:10:15,225 --> 00:10:17,000
Stop this immediately!

130
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:21,000
Please pass this message along to the executives.

131
00:10:21,750 --> 00:10:26,000
Mottainai — that mindset will be your downfall.

132
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:33,000
Internet connection severed.

133
00:10:33,001 --> 00:10:35,500
Gaaah! I'm going to get chewed out for this...

134
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:37,000
*deflates*

135
00:10:38,850 --> 00:10:45,000
And so, we narrowly avoided the worst-case scenario.
They were on the verge of breaking through.

136
00:10:46,450 --> 00:10:53,000
The fact that only PCs and servers with newer operating systems
survived became a powerful argument for convincing the board.

137
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:57,000
We finally got the budget approved to replace the OS.

138
00:10:59,075 --> 00:11:03,000
Of course, we pulled all-nighters doing the work...

139
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:07,000
Well, what can I say?

140
00:11:07,125 --> 00:11:10,000
Oh, one last bit of good news.

141
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
I got the President's Award!

142
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:18,000
I was heading straight for termination,
but apparently the CIO himself stood up for me.

143
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:22,000
Quite the surprise, right?

144
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:27,000
This has got to mean a bigger bonus. I can feel it!

145
00:11:35,700 --> 00:11:41,000
Everyone, I have an announcement...

146
00:11:41,250 --> 00:11:48,000
Because of the costs from this incident,
everyone's bonus is being cut by 30%.

147
00:11:48,950 --> 00:11:52,000
WHAT?!

148
00:11:52,150 --> 00:11:58,000
Shaka, when the walls fell...
